          "text": "     1→# NestJS MCP Server with MCP-Nest\n     2→\n     3→A NestJS-based MCP (Model Context Protocol) server built using **[MCP-Nest](https://github.com/rekog-labs/MCP-Nest)** that exposes tools for testing remote MCP tool integration with use-ai.\n     4→\n     5→## Overview\n     6→\n     7→This server uses **MCP-Nest decorators** (`@Tool`) to define tools in a type-safe, declarative way. It exposes the native MCP protocol over HTTP:\n     8→\n     9→### MCP Protocol Endpoints (JSON-RPC)\n    10→- `POST /mcp` - MCP JSON-RPC endpoint (supports `tools/list`, `tools/call`)\n    11→- `GET /sse` - Server-Sent Events for streaming\n    12→- `GET /mcp` - Session management (GET)\n    13→- `DELETE /mcp` - Session cleanup (DELETE)\n    14→- `POST /messages` - Message handling\n    15→\n    16→## Available Tools\n    17→\n    18→All tools are defined using MCP-Nest's `@Tool` decorator with Zod schemas:\n    19→\n    20→1. **add** - Add two numbers together\n    21→2. **multiply** - Multiply two numbers\n    22→3. **greet** - Greet a person by name\n    23→4. **get_weather** - Get mock weather data for a location\n    24→\n    25→## Usage\n    26→\n    27→### Development\n    28→```bash\n    29→bun run dev\n    30→```\n    31→\n    32→### Production\n    33→```bash\n    34→bun run start\n    35→```\n    36→\n    37→The server runs on port 3002 by default (configurable via `MCP_PORT` environment variable).\n    38→\n    39→## MCP-Nest Integration\n    40→\n    41→Tools are defined using the `@Tool` decorator:\n    42→\n    43→```typescript\n    44→@Tool({\n    45→  name: 'add',\n    46→  description: 'Add two numbers together',\n    47→  parameters: z.object({\n    48→    a: z.number().describe('The first number'),\n    49→    b: z.number().describe('The second number'),\n    50→  }),\n    51→})\n    52→async add({ a, b }: { a: number; b: number }) {\n    53→  return {\n    54→    content: [{ type: 'text', text: JSON.stringify({ result: a + b }) }],\n    55→  };\n    56→}\n    57→```\n    58→\n    59→The `McpModule` is configured with stateless HTTP transport:\n    60→\n    61→```typescript\n    62→McpModule.forRoot({\n    63→  name: 'test-mcp-server',\n    64→  version: '1.0.0',\n    65→  streamableHttp: {\n    66→    enableJsonResponse: true,\n    67→    statelessMode: true,\n    68→  },\n    69→})\n    70→```\n    71→\n    72→## Integration with use-ai\n    73→\n    74→The server is configured in the use-ai example app's playwright config to:\n    75→1. Start automatically before running E2E tests\n    76→2. Provide remote tools to the UseAI server via the `MCP_ENDPOINT_URL` environment variable\n    77→3. Tools are namespaced with \"mcp_\" prefix (e.g., `mcp_add`, `mcp_multiply`)\n    78→\n    79→## Testing\n    80→\n    81→Remote MCP tools can be tested via:\n    82→1. The \"Remote MCP Tools\" page in the example app (http://localhost:3000/remote-mcp-tools)\n    83→2. E2E tests in `apps/example/test/remote-mcp-tools.e2e.test.ts`\n    84→3. Direct curl commands using MCP JSON-RPC:\n    85→   ```bash\n    86→   # List available tools\n    87→   curl -X POST http://localhost:3002/mcp \\\n    88→     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n    89→     -H \"Accept: application/json, text/event-stream\" \\\n    90→     -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"tools/list\",\"id\":1}'\n    91→\n    92→   # Call a tool\n    93→   curl -X POST http://localhost:3002/mcp \\\n    94→     -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n    95→     -H \"Accept: application/json, text/event-stream\" \\\n    96→     -d '{\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"tools/call\",\"params\":{\"name\":\"add\",\"arguments\":{\"a\":5,\"b\":3}},\"id\":2}'\n    97→   ```\n    98→\n    99→## Architecture\n   100→\n   101→- **ToolsService**: Contains tool implementations with `@Tool` decorators\n   102→- **McpModule**: Provides native MCP protocol support (JSON-RPC, SSE) via MCP-Nest\n   103→- **RemoteMcpToolsProvider**: UseAI server component that communicates with this server using MCP JSON-RPC\n   104→- Uses **NestJS 11** for dependency injection and modularity\n   105→- Uses **MCP-Nest 1.8.4** for complete MCP protocol implementation\n   106→\n   107→### Communication Flow\n   108→\n   109→1. UseAI server's `RemoteMcpToolsProvider` sends `tools/list` JSON-RPC request to `/mcp`\n   110→2. MCP-Nest discovers tools via `@Tool` decorators and returns tool schemas\n   111→3. When Claude calls a tool, `RemoteMcpToolsProvider` sends `tools/call` JSON-RPC request\n   112→4. MCP-Nest executes the decorated tool method and returns MCP-compliant response\n   113→5. Result is extracted and returned to Claude\n   114→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n",
